course: biology
lesson: 112
title: Training and enrichment: keeping zoo animals busy and healthy
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A wild animal spends most of its day busy: searching for food, digging, climbing, sniffing and chasing. In a zoo the food comes easily, so keepers have to give back the busy part of life, and that is called enrichment.
explain: Enrichment can be food hidden in a cardboard box for a bear to rip open, a tray of sand with mealworms for meerkats to dig, new ropes for a gibbon, a new smell for a lion, or a meat-scented sack zooming along a zip wire for a tiger to chase.
explain: Training uses rewards, never force: the keeper clicks a clicker at the exact moment the animal does the right thing, then gives a treat, just as you might teach a dog to sit.
explain: It is not for tricks: an elephant that lifts its ear on cue lets the vet take blood without a fight, and an animal that touches a ball on a stick can be guided onto the scales.
fact: In target training an animal learns to touch a target, such as a ball on a stick, to get a reward, so keepers can move it onto scales and into position for health checks without force.
fact: A clicker marks the exact moment an animal does the right thing and a food reward follows; training with rewards is called positive reinforcement, and it is used for dogs too, and even fish and crocodiles have learned to come to a target for a reward.
fact: Modern zoos let animals choose whether to take part in training, and animals take part more often when they know they can walk away.
fact: Food enrichment hides food in boxes, logs, ice blocks or puzzle feeders, so a bear uses its nose, claws and strength as in the wild.
fact: Scent enrichment brings new smells such as spices, perfumes or the dung of other animals; lions given zebra dung roll in it and sniff it for a long time.
fact: New climbing frames, ropes, branches and hiding places are enrichment too, because new paths keep animals active and curious.
fact: Some zoos try music, sounds or screens for apes and parrots, and orangutans in some zoos have used tablets to watch videos and play games.
fact: Zoos test whether enrichment works by counting behaviour such as pacing (walking the same path again and again, often a sign of boredom or stress), play and resting before and after; this is part of animal welfare science.
fact: Meerkats dig all day in the wild, so a box of sand with hidden mealworms lets them do what they feel driven to do.
fact: Elephants are trained to present an ear, foot or trunk, so vets can take blood from the big veins behind the ear and test water blown out of the trunk for germs.
fact: Some zoos pull a lure along a rope or zip wire so tigers and cheetahs can stalk and sprint, which gives them exercise they are built for.
fact: Enrichment is planned on a calendar so that it is different every day.
exercise: What is target training? | The animal learns to touch a target, like a ball on a stick, for a reward | The keeper throws balls at the animal | The animal is shot with a dart to calm it down before each and every health check | Visitors aim at targets near the cage
exercise: Why does the keeper click the clicker at one exact moment? | It marks the very action that earns the reward, so it learns faster | The click hurts the animal's ears a little, so it obeys to stop the noise | The click tells visitors to clap | Animals like the noise more than food
exercise: What is training with rewards called? | Positive reinforcement | Punishment training | Natural selection | Hibernation
exercise: What happens in a modern zoo if an animal walks away from a training session? | It is allowed to choose | It gets no food at all for a whole week | The keeper drags it back | It is sent to another zoo
exercise: Why do keepers hide fish in a cardboard box for a bear? | It lets the bear use its nose, claws and strength as in the wild | Bears cannot eat fish from a bowl | The box keeps the fish cold and fresh for a whole month or even longer | Bears like to eat the cardboard best
exercise: Why would keepers bring zebra dung into the lion enclosure? | New smells are like new stories for an animal that lives by smell | To feed the lions with it | To trick the lions into thinking it is night so that they sleep longer | To clean the lions' fur
exercise: A gibbon gets new ropes and branches. Why does that count as enrichment? | New paths to explore keep it active and curious | Gibbons eat ropes for fibre | It stops the gibbon from ever climbing high up again | The old ropes were only decoration
exercise: How have orangutans in some zoos used tablets? | To watch videos and play games | To order their own food online each day | To phone their keepers at night | They break every tablet at once
exercise: How can scientists tell if an animal enjoys music? | Watch and count its behaviour before and after the music | Ask the animal to fill in a form with pictures after the music | See if it sings along | Check if it buys a ticket
exercise: A keeper notices an animal paces less after enrichment. What does that suggest? | The animal is doing better, with fewer repeated behaviours | The animal has become ill | The enrichment made it so bored that it stopped moving about | Pacing is a sign of happiness
exercise: Why give meerkats a box of sand with mealworms hidden in it? | Meerkats dig all day in the wild, and the box lets them do it | Meerkats need sand to brush their teeth | Sand keeps the mealworms from escaping and turning into beetles | The sand is their main food
exercise: Why do elephants learn to lift an ear for the vet? | Big veins behind the ear make taking blood easy | The vet checks for earwax each day | It cools the vet down | Elephants hear the vet better with one big ear lifted up
exercise: What is an elephant trunk wash for? | Testing water blown out of the trunk for germs | Cleaning the trunk so it looks smart before a show | Teaching the elephant to swim | Making the trunk longer
exercise: Why do some zoos pull a meat-scented lure along a zip wire for a tiger? | It lets the tiger stalk and sprint, as it is built to | It teaches the tiger to fly | It is the only way tigers can eat | It frightens the tiger into resting quietly for the rest of the day
exercise: Why is enrichment planned on a calendar? | So it is different every day and stays interesting | So the animals know the date | So keepers can do exactly the same thing every day forever | Calendars are needed for zoo licences
exercise: «4 meerkats digging in the sand box, how many ears is that lot» | 8, two ears each | 4, only one ear each | 16, four ears each | 6, they share some
test: «just smack it with a stick if it won't do what u want, works on my brother lol» What do good zoos really do? | Use rewards only and let the animal choose to take part | Use a stick but only gently | Shout until the animal obeys | Starve the animal for a few days until it finally obeys you
test: Why would choice make training easier for an animal? | It feels less stressed and is more willing to come back | Choice makes animals forget everything | Animals hate being given choices | Choice means the animal never trains at all, so nothing is learned
test: Which is the best example of useful training, not a trick? | An elephant lifting its foot so the vet can check it | A sea lion balancing a ball on its nose for a crowd | A parrot saying «hello» to visitors | A bear dancing to music
test: What would happen to a cheetah that never chased anything for years? | It could get unfit and bored | It would run even faster than before | It would turn into a different cat | Nothing, cheetahs are built to sit still
test: «what's the point of new climbing frames, monkeys don't care» Which answer is right? | New paths to explore keep them active and curious, like a new playground for you | Monkeys only climb when people are watching, so frames are just for show | Climbing frames are for the keepers | Monkeys hate change of any kind
test: Even fish and crocodiles have been trained to come to a target. What does this show? | Many kinds of animals can learn from rewards, not only clever mammals | Fish are smarter than elephants | Crocodiles love being stroked | Only cold-blooded animals can learn from rewards, while mammals never can
test: In Kung Fu Panda, Po trains by doing kung fu moves for dumplings. What is true about real giant pandas in zoos? | Keepers train them with treats for health checks, not for fighting | Real pandas really do learn kung fu | Real pandas are trained by being hit with sticks until they fight well | Real pandas cannot learn anything at all
test: Which question would you ask to test if a puzzle feeder is too hard for a bear? | Does the bear give up and walk away before it gets the food? | Is the puzzle painted a nice bright colour that the bear might like? | Did the puzzle cost a lot? | Does the bear sleep next to it?
test: «2 bears and 1 tiger at the enrichment day, how many legs, no wait add the other tiger» | 16, four legs each | 12, four legs each for 3 animals | 8, two legs each for 4 animals | 14, the tiger has two legs
test: Why do rewards usually work better than punishments for teaching animals? | The animal wants to repeat what brought a reward and is not afraid | Punished animals always learn ten times faster than the rewarded ones | Rewards make animals forget the lesson | Animals cannot feel fear at all
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the practice of target training in zoological institutions? | An animal is rewarded for touching a designated target, permitting movement without coercion | An animal is compelled by force to approach a designated location | Visitors are permitted to throw objects at a target within the enclosure | An animal is sedated in order to be weighed
exam: Identify the principal function of the audible signal produced by a clicker during training. | It marks precisely the moment of the correct behaviour, prior to reward | It administers a mild discomfort to the ear in order to deter the animal from error | It summons the visiting public to the enclosure | It replaces the provision of food entirely
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the training of elephants for veterinary procedures. | Elephants are trained to present the ear, permitting blood to be drawn from veins behind it | Elephants are restrained by chains for all veterinary procedures | Veterinary procedures upon elephants are prohibited by law | Blood is customarily drawn from the tip of the trunk
exam: Ascertain the method by which the efficacy of enrichment is customarily evaluated. | By the recording of behaviours such as pacing, play and rest before and after its provision | By the solicitation of written opinions from members of the visiting public at the enclosure | By the weighing of the enrichment devices after use | By the assessment of the cost of each device
